<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pha That Luang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1566</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Great Stupa of Vientiane</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pha That Luang is a large gold-covered Buddhist stupa in Vientiane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, built by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a> around 1566 on the site of an older shrine. It was damaged in the Siamese sack of Vientiane in 1828 and restored in the 20th century. Considered the national symbol of Laos, it appears on the country's emblem and is the focus of a major annual festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pha-that-luang.jpg" alt="Pha That Luang" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Coalfruit1012 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pha_That_Luang,_July_2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
